\id ROM - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.13
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Romans
\toc1 Paul’s letter to the believers in Rome
\toc2 Romans
\mt2 Paul’s letter to the believers in
\mt1 Rome
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\p
\v 1 \add This letter is from\add* Paul, a slave of Messiah Jesus and a chosen ambassador who’s been set apart for God’s good message,
\v 2 which God had previously promised through \add the messages\add* of his prophets in the holy scriptures.
\p
\v 3 \add This good message\add* is about his son, who was conceived as a descendant of David,
\v 4 and became known as God’s son according to the spirit of holiness—demonstrating power by coming back to life from the dead. Yes, he’s our master, Jesus Messiah,
\v 5 and it’s through him that we received generous gifts, and \add also\add* the assignment to faithfully, obediently \add take\add* his name to all the non-Jews,
\v 6 which also includes you non-Jews there who Jesus Messiah invited.
\p
\v 7 \add This letter is written\add* to all those in Rome that God loves and considers as holy. \add May\add* you all experience generosity and peace from God our father and \add from\add* \add the\add* master Jesus Messiah.
\s1 Paul’s wish to visit Rome
\p
\v 8 I’ll start by thanking my God through Jesus Messiah for all of you, because the \add story about\add* your faith has been told in many countries.
\v 9 Yes, the God that I serve in my spirit, in the good message about his son, is my witness about how I never stop mentioning you \add Romans\add*
\v 10 in my prayers—asking that somehow God will favour me by allowing me to come and visit you,
\v 11 because I’m longing to see you so that I can share a particular spiritual gift with you in order to strengthen you.
\v 12 I want \add all of\add* us to be encouraged together by each other’s faith—both me and you \add Romans\add*.
\p
\v 13 Brothers and sisters, I don’t want you to be unaware that I’d planned several times to come to you, but I was vetoed until recently so that I’d also have a harvest among you similar to those among the other non-Jews.
\v 14 Whether it’s Greeks or other foreigners, wise people or foolish, I have an obligation,
\v 15 and so I’m also eager to talk about the good message to all of you there in Rome.
\p
\v 16 I’m not ashamed to talk about the good message, because it \add conveys\add* God’s power to save everyone who believes it—first Jews and then non-Jews.
\v 17 It explains how God makes us guiltless—by faith \add leading\add* to \add more\add* faith—as it’s been written: Godly people will live by faith.
\s1 God’s anger towards those who deny his existence
\p
\v 18 God’s severe anger is being revealed from heaven on the ungodliness and disobedience of people who limit the truth by \add their\add* disobedience,
\v 19 because the fact that there is a god has been shown to them, because God \add himself\add* has revealed it.
\v 20 Eber since the creation of the world, God’s invisible attributes have been clearly seen through his workmanship—both his eternal power and his divinity—so \add people\add* have no excuse.
\v 21 Even though they know \add about\add* God, they didn’t honour or thank him, but their theories made them incompetent and their minds turned from perception towards darkness.
\v 22 They claimed to be scientific, but instead they believed foolishness,
\v 23 and \add so\add* they changed the greatness of the eternal God into the likeness of an image of a mortal human, or birds or animals or reptiles.
\p
\v 24 Therefore God reluctantly let their lusts lead to impurity and the dishonouring of their bodies among themselves,
\v 25 as they exchanged God’s truth for a lie, and worshipped and served the creation, instead of the creator, who \add should be\add* praised throughout all time. May it be so.
\p
\v 26 Because of that, God reluctantly let them exercise their dishonourable passions, and their women exchanged natural behaviours for unnatural ones,
\v 27 and similarly their men deserted the natural behaviours towards women, and stoked their lusts towards each other—man with man, and the shame that it produces, and then received back in themselves the payment which is appropriate for such deception.
\p
\v 28 They didn’t want to know about God, and so he reluctantly let their depraved minds be filled with unhelpful thoughts,
\v 29 and so they were filled with disobedience, wickedness, greed, and malice, and full of envy, murder, strife, and deceit, \add becoming\add* malicious people, gossips,
\v 30 slanderers, god-haters, abusers, proud people, braggers, and inventors of evil, unable to be persuaded by their parents,
\v 31 unintelligent, untrustworthy, unloving, \add and\add* unmerciful.
\v 32 They know that God must exercise justice towards people doing such things, yet they not only practice those things which will lead to a death sentence, but they also approve when others do them.
\c 2
\s1 Judgement even for those without the written law
\p
\v 1 So there’s no excuse for anyone who judges others, because you’re condemning yourselves if you’re doing those same things that you’re judging others for,
\v 2 because we know that God’s judgement is based on the factual accounts of what \add each person\add* is doing.
\v 3 But maybe you’re thinking that you can judge others doing these things and yet \add somehow\add* escape God’s judgement when you do them yourself?
\v 4 Or maybe you’re taking advantage of God’s kindness and patience, without realising that his kindness was \add meant\add* to lead you to turn away from your wrongdoing?
\v 5 But it’s your hardness and failure to turn away from wrongdoing that’s \add causing\add* severe anger to be stored up for God’s judgement day, when \add all\add* will be revealed, \add including\add* his severe anger,
\v 6 as each person will be \add judged\add* by their actions.
\v 7 Those who’ve persistently done good, expecting praise and honour, and to escape destruction, will be given eternal life,
\v 8 but those who’ve \add lived out\add* their selfish ambition and refused to believe the truth, but \add let themselves get\add* persuaded by disobedience, \add will meet with\add* severe anger and rage.
\v 9 For every person doing evil, there’ll be trouble and distress—to Jews first and then to non-Jews,
\v 10 but for those who do good, there’ll be praise and honour and peace—to Jews first and then to non-Jews,
\v 11 because God doesn’t \add indulge in\add* favouritism.
\p
\v 12 Those who weren’t under the \add Jewish\add* laws and yet sinned, they’ll be judged without the law, and those who knew the laws and sinned will be judged by the law,
\v 13 because listening to the law doesn’t make \add people\add* right with God, but rather it’s those who obey the law who’ll be declared guiltless.
\v 14 Whenever other nations don’t have \add the Jewish\add* laws, but naturally obey what’s in the laws, \add they show that\add* even without our laws, they have their own law.
\v 15 They demonstrate that they effectively have God’s law written in their minds and agreeing with their consciences, and between the two they’ll be either accused or defended
\v 16 on the day when God, through Messiah Jesus, judges people’s private \add lives\add*, as I’ve shared \add as part of\add* the good message.
\s1 Jews who disobey their laws
\p
\v 17 But if you’re a Jew, and trusting in the law, and boasting about \add being chosen by\add* God
\v 18 and knowing his will and knowing what’s important because of familiarity with the law,
\v 19 and if you’ve persuaded yourself that you’re a guide for blind people and a light to people who are in darkness,
\v 20 and that you’re an instructor to foolish people and a teacher of children, and you appear to be knowledgeable about the truth of the law,
\v 21 then why do you teach others when you haven’t \add even\add* taught yourself? You announce that stealing is wrong, but you’re a thief.
\v 22 You teach about not messing with other people’s spouses, yet you do it yourself. You detest idols, but you sneak money out of the temple.
\v 23 You boast about \add being a student\add* of the law, but you dishonour God by breaking the laws.
\v 24 \add Yes,\add* it’s been written: ‘God’s name is slandered among the nations because of you \add people\add*.’
\p
\v 25 Circumcision is of value if you’re keeping the law, but if you’re breaking the laws, then your circumcision means just the same as if you’re uncircumcised,
\v 26 so if uncircumcised \add people groups\add* behave justly according to the law, even though they’re not physically circumcised, \add God\add* will consider them as his people.
\v 27 \add Yes, those people groups\add* who aren’t circumcised, but keep the law, will be judging you who were given the law in writing and are circumcised, yet break those laws.
\v 28 A \add true\add* Jew isn’t just a Jew on the outside, and their circumcision isn’t just on the outside of their body,
\v 29 but they’re a Jew even in their private life and through circumcision of their heart—by \add the\add* spirit and not by the letter of the law—earning praise not from people, but from God.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 So what advantage is there in being a Jew, and what’s the benefit of circumcision?
\v 2 A lot, in every way. Firstly, Jews were entrusted with \add the writing down\add* of God’s messages.
\v 3 But what if some didn’t believe? Wouldn’t the unbelief of Jews make faith \add that comes from\add* God worthless?
\v 4 Absolutely not. Even if every person was a liar, God is truthful, just like it’s been written \add about him\add*:
\q1 ‘You will be proved right about what you say,
\q2 and you’ll prevail when you’re judged \add in court\add*.’
\p
\v 5 But if our sinfulness highlights God’s sinlessness, what can we say about that? Isn’t God, the one inflicting the severe anger, wrong \add to punish us\add*? (Of course, this is reasoning from a human perspective.)
\v 6 Of course not, otherwise how could God be able to judge the world?
\p
\v 7 But if my lie \add exposes\add* God’s truth and so brings him honour, why would he still judge me as a sinner?
\v 8 Some people falsely accuse us of saying, ‘Let’s do evil things so that good might follow.’ Those who say that deserve their punishment.
\s1 No person is guiltless
\p
\v 9 What then? Do Jews have any advantage? Definitely not, because we showed earlier that both Jews and other nationalities are all under sin’s \add shadow\add*,
\v 10 just like it was written long ago:
\q1 ‘No one is guiltless,
\q2 not even one person.
\q1
\v 11 No one understands,
\q2 there’s not even one person trying to find God.
\q1
\v 12 Everyone has turned away,
\q2 it was a waste of time.
\q1 No one is being kind,
\q2 Not even one person.’
\q1
\v 13 ‘Their words lead to death,
\q2 their tongues are deceptive.’
\q1 ‘Their lips spray out poison.’
\q1
\v 14 ‘Their mouths are full of cursing and bitterness.’
\q1
\v 15 ‘They rush off to go and shed blood.
\q2
\v 16 They leave trails of destruction and misery—
\q2
\v 17 they don’t know the path \add that leads to\add* peace.’
\q2
\v 18 Revering God isn’t at the front of their \add minds\add*.
\p
\v 19 We know that whatever the law says, it’s for \add everyone\add* that the law applies to, so that no one can speak against it, and the whole world will be bound by God’s judgement.
\v 20 No human will be declared guiltless in front of God by their keeping the law, because it’s the law that teaches what sin is.
\s1 Removing the guilt from sin
\p
\v 21 But now God has revealed a way to remove guilt that’s not by rules—a way that Moses and the prophets wrote about.
\v 22 We can be made right with God through faith in Jesus Messiah. This is for \em everyone\em* who believes, because there’s no distinction \add between Jews\add* \add and others\add*,
\v 23 because everyone has sinned and is unable to be honoured by God.
\v 24 \add But\add* even without deserving it, we can be declared guiltless by God’s generosity, by being bought out of slavery \add to sin\add* by Messiah Jesus,
\v 25 who God placed \add on the altar\add* as a sacrifice for sin. \add This happens\add* through faith in his blood, which was a sign of his sinlessness.
\p (God hasn’t yet judged those who sinned in the past,
\v 26 because of his patience, waiting for the present time to show his righteousness and to be fair, and to remove the guilt of those with faith in Jesus.)
\p
\v 27 So where does that leave boasting? It’s not in the picture. How come? Through obeying Moses’s laws? No, through a law about faith.
\v 28 So now we should expect that a person will be made guiltless by faith—not by obeying Moses’s laws.
\v 29 Or is God only god of the Jews? Isn’t he also the god of others? Yes, he is the god of others.
\v 30 Indeed, there’s only one God, and he declares people guiltless by faith whether they’re circumcised Jews, or non-Jews.
\v 31 So does this ‘faith’ cancel the law? Not on your life. In fact, it upholds the law.
\c 4
\s1 Abraham’s faith
\p
\v 1 So what did our physical ancestor, Abraham, discover about all this?
\v 2 Because if Abraham was declared guiltless because of his actions, maybe he could boast? But not towards God,
\v 3 because the scriptures say: ‘Abraham believed in God, so he was considered guiltless.’
\v 4 Now for someone who’s working, their wages are considered ‘owed’, not a gift.
\v 5 However, someone who’s not working, but having faith that God will declare disobedient people as guiltless, that person’s faith is considered to make them guiltless.
\v 6 It’s just like what David said about blessing a person who God declares as guiltless despite their actions:
\q1
\v 7 ‘Those who’ve been forgiven for their lawlessness will be happy,
\q2 whose sins have been covered over.
\q1
\v 8 Anyone who Yahweh declares to have no sin will be happy.’
\p
\v 9 Is this favour shown \add only\add* to circumcised Jews, or is it also for non-Jews? After all, we were saying that Abraham was considered guiltless because of his faith.
\v 10 So how did that happen? Because he was circumcised, or when he was \add still\add* uncircumcised? No, it was before he was circumcised.
\v 11 He received circumcision as a sign—proof of the righteousness that he gained from his faith while he was still uncircumcised—becoming the father of everyone who’s uncircumcised but believes so that they’ll be declared guiltless.
\v 12 So our ancestor Abraham is the ancestor of us as a circumcised people group, not just because we’re circumcised, but also as we follow the steps of faith he took while uncircumcised.
\s1 God’s promise is attained through faith
\p
\v 13 It wasn’t because of \add his obeying\add* the law that it was promised that Abraham or his descendants would inherit the world, but because of his righteousness \add that came from\add* his faith,
\v 14 because if the ones \add obeying\add* the law \add can become\add* heirs, then faith would be declared as useless, and the promise would be invalidated,
\v 15 because the law produces severe anger, and where there’s no law, there’s nothing to break.
\p
\v 16 Because of that, the promise to the descendants is by faith—\add given\add* not only to the ‘descendants of the law’, but also to the ‘descendants of Abraham’s faith’. This promise is given as a gift, and has been confirmed,
\v 17 as it’s been written, ‘I’ve appointed you as a father of many nations.’ He was appointed in front of the god he believed in—the god who brings the dead \add back\add* to life, and who \add creates by\add* speaking things into being that didn’t previously exist.
\v 18 \add Without any other evidence\add*, he believed in hope itself, and thus he became the father of many nations, just like he’d been told: ‘Your descendants will be like \add the sand and the stars\add*.’
\v 19 He didn’t falter in his faith, seeing that his body was already infertile, being about a hundred years old, and with Sarah being past menopause.
\v 20 He didn’t show unbelief and doubt God’s promise when it was given, but rather he was strengthened in his faith and praised God,
\v 21 being quite certain that what God promises, he’s also able to do.
\v 22 Therefore \add his faith\add* was credited to him and he was declared guiltless.
\v 23 However, that statement wasn’t written about Abraham only,
\v 24 but about us, too, who are also going to receive the same credit, because of our faith in the God who brought our master Jesus back to life from the dead
\v 25 after he was handed over \add to the executioners\add* because of our sins, and he was brought back to life to make us guiltless.
\c 5
\s1 God showed his love to sinners
\p
\v 1 So now because we’ve been declared guiltless by our faith, we can have peace with God through our master Jesus Messiah.
\v 2 Through him we also have access by faith into this generosity that we \add depend on\add*, and we’re boasting in hope of God’s glory. WHAT DOES THIS MEAN?
\v 3 Not only that, but we’re also boasting even though we’re \add still\add* being persecuted, because we know that difficulties produce endurance,
\v 4 and endurance shows who we really are, and knowing that produces hope,
\v 5 and we’re not disgraced by that hope, because God’s love has been poured out into our hearts through \add the\add* holy spirit who’s been given to us.
\p
\v 6 While we were still weak, and at the \add right\add* time for ungodly \add people\add*, \add the\add* messiah died for us.
\v 7 Now it’s rare for someone to \add offer to\add* die to save a guiltless person, although perhaps someone might offer for the \add greater\add* good,
\v 8 but God demonstrated his love towards us in that \add the\add* messiah died for us \em while we were still sinners\em*!
\p
\v 9 So now that we’ve been declared guiltless because of his blood, we’ll certainly be spared from his severe anger,
\v 10 because if we were reconciled to God through the \em death\em* of his son \em while we were still enemies\em*, now that we’ve been reconciled, how much more certain that we’ll be saved by Jesus’ \em life\em*!
\v 11 And not only that, because of what our master Jesus Messiah \add has done\add* so that we might receive that reconciliation, we can now boast in God about it.
\s1 Death through Adam but life through Jesus
\p
\v 12 Sin came into the world through one man, and death came as a result of sin, so death spread to everyone because everyone has disobeyed God.
\v 13 Sin was in the world before Moses’ law, but it wasn’t being charged against them, because the law had not yet \add been given\add*.
\v 14 But death reigned from Adam’s time until Moses’s time, even over those who didn’t sin in the way that Adam did—Adam\f + \fr 5:14 \ft Adam is called ‘the son of God’ in Luke 3:38.\f* who was a pattern of the \add messiah\add* who would \add eventually\add* come.
\p
\v 15 But the gift is not like the disobedience, because if the disobedience of one man introduced death for many, God’s generosity and the gift that comes from that generosity is much more. That gift is the one man Jesus Messiah, who’s been given to very many \add people\add*.
\v 16 It only took one man who disobeyed \add to lead to\add* condemnation, and one act of disobedience \add to lead to\add* judgement, but the gift is to bring guiltlessness despite \em many\em* acts of disobedience.
\v 17 Because if the disobedience of one man led to death reigning \add over us\add* through that one man, how much more will the ones receiving an abundance of generosity and the gift of righteousness be reigning in life through the one man Jesus Messiah.
\p
\v 18 So just as one act of disobedience \add led\add* all people to condemnation, so also one action \add brought\add* all people to guiltlessness \add and hence to\add* life.
\v 19 The disobedience of one man caused many people to be made sinners, so also the obedience of one man caused many people to be made guiltless.
\p
\v 20 The law was introduced so that acts of disobedience would be plentiful, but where sin has been plentiful, generosity has overflowed,
\v 21 so that just as sin reigned in death, so also generosity \add now\add* reigns through righteousness, \add leading to\add* eternal life through our master Jesus Messiah.
\c 6
\s1 Dying and coming back to life with Jesus
\p
\v 1 So should we continue disobeying so that more of God’s generosity \add will be displayed\add*?
\v 2 Not on your life! We who died to sin, how could we \add go back\add* to living in it?
\v 3 Or didn’t you realise that when we were immersed into Messiah Jesus, we were immersed into his death?
\v 4 Therefore we were buried with him through the immersion into death, so that just as Messiah was brought back to life by the father’s power, so too we can live a new life.
\v 5 If we’ve taken part in the likeness of his death, then we’ll also \add take part\add* in the resurrection,
\v 6 knowing that the old personality was executed with him so that sin would have no \add more\add* power over our bodies, and hence we no longer need to serve it,
\v 7 because the personality that’s died has been declared free from sin.
\v 8 So if we died with Messiah, we also believe that we’ll live with him,
\v 9 knowing that now that Messiah has come back to life from the dead, he won’t die again—death has no more power over him.
\v 10 When he died, he died to sin once and for all, but now that he lives, he lives to \add please\add* God.
\v 11 So too, you should consider yourselves dead to sin, and at the same time, alive to God in Messiah Jesus.
\p
\v 12 So therefore don’t let sin rule over your mortal bodies in such a way that you’re submitting to its lusts.
\v 13 Don’t use any parts of your bodies to sin, as if they’re weapons of disobedience, but instead present yourselves to God as if you came back to life from the dead, and use all your body parts for God, as if they’re weapons of righteousness,
\v 14 so that sin won’t take control of you—because you’re not under law, but under generosity.
\s1 Now we are slaves to righteousness
\p
\v 15 What could that mean? That we can sin \add freely\add* because we’re not under law but under generosity? Not on your life!
\v 16 Don’t you know who you’re presenting yourselves to as obedient slaves? \add Yes,\add* whoever you submit to, you’re their slaves—either slaves of sin that leads to death, or slaves of obedience that leads to righteousness.
\v 17 But be thankful to God that \add although\add* you were slaves to sin, your hearts submitted to what was taught,
\v 18 and \add so\add*, having been freed from sin, you became slaves of righteousness.
\v 19 I’m speaking from a human perspective because of your \add human\add* weakness. Just as you previously used parts of your bodies as slaves to impurity and lawlessness, leading \add down to further\add* lawlessness, so now present your body parts as slaves to righteousness, leading \add up\add* to holiness.
\p
\v 20 When you were slaves of sin, you were immune to righteousness,
\v 21 and so what was the fruit you had back then which you’re now ashamed of? The end \add result\add* of that \add lifestyle\add* is death.
\v 22 But now that you’re free from \add slavery to\add* sin, and are God’s slaves \add instead\add*, you bear the fruit of holiness, and the end \add result\add* of that is living forever,
\v 23 because the wages of sin are death, but God gives eternal life as a gift through our master Jesus Messiah.
\c 7
\s1 Becoming dead to the Law
\p
\v 1 Brothers and sisters, you know the Law \add well\add* that I’m talking about, so surely you realise that the law acts as a person’s master for as long as they live?
\v 2 For \add example\add*, a woman who’s married to her husband is bound by the law, but if her husband dies then she’s released from the law concerning her husband.
\v 3 So while her husband was still living, he’d call her an adulteress if she \add slept\add* with another man, but after her husband is deceased, she’s free from the law and doesn’t become an adulteress if she \add marries\add* another man.
\v 4 So, my brothers and sisters, you, too, were made dead to the law through the body of the messiah so that you could \add join\add* to another—to the one who came back to life from the dead—so that we can bear fruit for God.
\v 5 When we were \add still\add* focused on the physical, the passions \add aroused\add* by sinning against the law were working in our body parts so that the fruit would be death,
\v 6 but now that we’re released from the law which held us—released by dying to it—we can serve in new ways \add guided by the\add* spirit instead of by the letter \add of the law\add*.
\s1 God’s Law reveals our sin
\p
\v 7 So what will we say then? That the law is sin? Not on your life! I wouldn’t have known what sin was if it wasn’t for the Law. \add For example,\add* I wouldn’t have known what coveting was if the Law hadn’t told me not to covet.
\v 8 But sin took the opportunity from \add that\add* command to produce covetousness in me, because sin would be dead without the Law.
\v 9 For a time I was living without the Law, but once God’s command came, sin revived
\v 10 and I died, and I discovered that the command, which \add should lead to\add* life, \add led\add* to death.
\v 11 Sin deceived me when the command gave it an opportunity, and in doing so, it killed me.
\p
\v 12 Now the Law is indeed holy, and God’s command is holy and righteous and good,
\v 13 so did what’s good turn into death? Not on your life! Rather, it was the good \add Law\add* that allowed me to see sin as sin and a producer of death, and the command showed me how completely sinful sin really is.
\s1 Doing what we know is wrong
\p
\v 14 We know that God’s law is spiritual, but I am physical, having been \add born\add*\f + \fr 7:14 \ft Paul actually talks about ‘being sold into sin’ here which is slavery language.\f* into sin.
\v 15 I don’t know what’s going on, because I do what I don’t want to do, and I \add end up\add* doing what I hate.
\v 16 \add So\add* if I do something that I didn’t want to do, then I’m agreeing with the Law that it’s good,
\v 17 and it’s no longer me that’s doing it, but the sin that’s in me.
\v 18 I’m aware that good isn’t in my physical body, because I want to do good but I can’t.
\v 19 I don’t do the good that I want to do, but rather I do the evil that I don’t want to do,
\v 20 so if I’m doing what I don’t want to do, then it’s not me doing it, but it’s the sin in me that does it.
\p
\v 21 As a result, I’ve discovered the principle that when I want to do good, \add actually\add* there’s evil in me,
\v 22 because I’m happily accepting God’s Law on the inside,
\v 23 but I’m observing another law in my body parts, fighting against my mind and keeping me imprisoned in sin’s law, which is in my body parts.
\v 24 I’m a miserable person. Who’ll rescue me from this body that’s \add full of\add* death?
\v 25 Thankfully, it’s God, through our master Jesus Messiah. So I end up with my mind \add wanting to\add* serve God’s Law, but on the other hand, my body wants to serve sin’s law.
\c 8
\s1 Being spiritually aware
\p
\v 1 So now those who are in Messiah Jesus cannot be condemned,\f + \fr 8:1 \ft Note that many older translations included text here that came from manuscripts from later centuries and that isn’t there in the oldest manuscripts, i.e., the additional text appears to have been added by ‘helpful’ copyists but was not original to Shaul/Paul.\f*
\v 2 because the law of the spirit of life in him freed you from the law of sin and death.
\v 3 The law was powerless in that it was faltering through our bodies. God sent his son in a body just like our sinful bodies, and concerning sin, he condemned sin \add itself\add* in the body,
\v 4 so that for those of us who are no longer governed by physical desires, but by \add the\add* spirit, the law’s just action will be fulfilled in us.
\v 5 People who are governed by their physical desires think about physical things, but those who are governed by the spirit think about spiritual things.
\v 6 The worldly mindset \add leads to\add* death, but the spiritual mindset \add leads to\add* life and peace.
\v 7 The worldly mindset is making God your enemy because it’s not being subjected to God’s law, in fact it’s not even able to,
\v 8 and those who are worldly aren’t able to do what pleases God.
\p
\v 9 And you, \add brothers and sisters,\add* aren’t worldly, but are in the spirit, if indeed God’s spirit is living in you, but anyone who doesn’t have Messiah’s spirit doesn’t belong to him.
\v 10 But if Messiah is in you, while your body is dead because of sin, your spirit has life because it’s \add been declared\add* guiltless.
\v 11 \add Yes,\add* if the spirit of the one who brought Jesus back to life is living in you, then the one who brought Messiah Jesus back to life will also give life to your mortal bodies by means of his spirit living inside you.
\p
\v 12 So then, brothers and sisters, we don’t owe anything to our sinful natures, nor do we need to live in worldly ways.
\v 13 If your lives, \add brothers and sisters\add*, are governed by your sinful natures, you’ll die, but if you put bodily practices to death with \add the help of the\add* spirit, you’ll live,
\v 14 because everyone who’s led by God’s spirit \add becomes one of\add* God’s children.
\v 15 You didn’t receive a spirit of slavery to fear again, but \add rather\add* it was \add the\add* spirit of adoption as children that you received, and \add with his help\add* we call out, ‘Dad, dad!’
\v 16 God’s spirit himself agrees together with our spirit that we are God’s children,
\v 17 and if we’re his children, then we’re also heirs, so we’ll receive an inheritance from God. That makes us fellow-heirs along with Messiah—if we’re suffering with him then we’ll also be honoured along with him.
\s1 Even creation itself is waiting
\p
\v 18 I’m sure that any sufferings \add we experience\add* in this age are nothing in comparison with the coming splendour that’s going to be revealed to us,
\v 19 because \add all of\add* creation is eagerly expecting and waiting for God’s children to be revealed.
\v 20 \add Yes,\add* all of creation has been subjected to lack of purpose, not willingly, but because it was decided by the one who subjected it, but \add it remains with\add* the hope
\v 21 that it too will be freed from the slavery of corruption, and then \add released\add* into the freedom of the splendour of God’s children,
\v 22 because we’re aware that all of creation is groaning and suffering\f + \fr 8:22 \ft The word picture here is one of childbirth.\f* along with us right through to the present time.
\v 23 And not only that, but we ourselves who have the initial tastes of the spirit \add life\add*, we’re also groaning and waiting like children for our bodies to be set free from slavery,
\v 24 because we were saved, having hope, but if you can see it then it’s not hope, because you don’t need to hope for something you can \add already\add* see.
\v 25 So we hope for what we can’t \add yet\add* see, and \add so\add* we have to patiently keep waiting.
\p
\v 26 Similarly, the spirit also helps \add us\add* with our weaknesses, because we don’t \add even\add* know what to pray for, but the spirit intercedes with groanings that we can’t put into words.
\v 27 The one who searches our hearts knows the mindset of the spirit, because the spirit intercedes for the believers as God desires.
\p
\v 28 We know that God makes all things work together for good for everyone who loves him—those who are being invited according to \add his\add* purpose.
\v 29 After all, those he knew in advance, he also determined in advance \add to be\add* similar to his son’s image, so that \add his son\add* would become the eldest of many siblings.
\v 30 The ones he’d determined in advance he also invited, and the ones he’d invited, he also declared guiltless, and the ones he declared guiltless, he also honoured \add in heaven\add*.
\s1 Nothing can separate us from God’s love
\p
\v 31 So then, what can we say about all this? If God is for us, who’s against us?
\v 32 If he didn’t spare his own son, but gave him \add to be executed\add* for us, won’t they together also give us everything else?
\v 33 Who’ll bring charges against people chosen by God? God is the one declaring \add us\add* guiltless.
\v 34 So who’s the one condemning \add us\add*? Messiah Jesus the one who died but came back to life and who’s \add now\add* \add sitting\add* beside God) is also pleading \em for\em* us.
\v 35 Who will separate us from Messiah’s love? Troubles or distress or persecution or famine or poverty or danger or execution?
\v 36 As it’s been written:
\q1 ‘We are being put to death all day
\q2 on your account.
\q1 We were counted like sheep \add ready to be\add* slaughtered.’
\m
\v 37 But in all that, we’re more than conquerors through the one who loved us.
\v 38 \add Yes,\add* I’ve been persuaded that neither death or life, angels or rulers, present or future events, powers,
\v 39 height or depth, or anything else in creation, will be able to separate us from God’s love that’s \add revealed\add* in our master Messiah Jesus.
\c 9
\s1 God’s right to choose
\p
\v 1 I’m not lying—I’m speaking truthfully in Messiah, and both my conscience and \add the\add* holy spirit agree
\v 2 that my sorrow is extensive, and the sorrow in my heart just won’t stop,
\v 3 because I was hoping that I myself would be a sacrifice from the messiah for my brothers and sisters, my fellow-citizens in this world.
\v 4 They’re the \add descendants\add* of Israel who’ve been adopted as \add God’s\add* children, and who \add God revealed\add* his power to, as well as the agreements, the Law, the \add temple\add* worship, and the promises.
\v 5 The patriarchs are Jewish, and the messiah, who has authority over everything, physically came from them. \add May God be\add* blessed throughout the ages. May it be so.
\p
\v 6 But it’s not that God’s message has failed. Israel doesn’t include all of Israel’s descendants,
\v 7 nor are all of Abraham’s descendants his children, but, ‘It’s through Isaac that your descendants will be counted.’
\v 8 That isn’t \add talking about\add* biological children—these are God’s children, but the children of the promise are being counted as descendants.
\v 9 After all, this message is the promise: ‘I’ll come back, and Sarah will have a son.’
\p
\v 10 And not only that, but also Rebecca, from one pregnancy to our ancestor Isaac, delivered \add twins\add*.
\v 11 Before they were born, and \add so\add* before they could do anything good or bad, in order that God’s deliberate choice would stand
\v 12 (not as a result of effort, but by God’s announcement), she was told, ‘The older child will serve the younger one.’
\v 13 As it was written: ‘I loved Jacob but I hated Esau.’
\p
\v 14 So what should we say then? Is God unfair? Of course not,
\v 15 because he said to Moses: ‘I’ll be merciful to whoever I want to, and I’ll have pity on anyone I want to.’
\v 16 In other words, it doesn’t depend on the willingness or activities of the person, but on God’s mercy.
\v 17 After all, in the scriptures, \add God\add* says to the king of Egypt: ‘I \add chose\add* you \add as king\add* so that I can demonstrate my power through you, and so that my name will be talked about all over the world.’
\v 18 In other words, God will be merciful to whoever he wants to, but he hardens whoever he wants to.
\s1 God’s anger and his mercy
\p
\v 19 So now you’ll ask me: Then how can God blame us, because how could anyone resist whatever he wants to do?
\v 20 Oh human, are you talking back to God? Can the creature that was made talk back to the maker and ask: ‘Why did you make me like this?’
\v 21 Doesn’t the potter have the right to split the lump of clay and make a beautiful bowl out of half and a \add chamber pot\add* out of the other half?
\p
\v 22 \add What\add* if God wanted to display his severe anger and to demonstrate his power, but then showed great patience to the objects of his severe anger that he’d prepared for destruction?
\v 23 \add What if he did that\add* in order to make the riches of his greatness known to the objects of his mercy that he’d prepared for honour?
\v 24 \add Yes,\add* he even called us, not only from \add among\add* the Jews but also from non-Jews.
\v 25 The \add prophet\add* Hosea even wrote:
\q1 “Those who aren’t my people
\q2 I will call ‘my people’,
\q1 and the \add woman\add* who was never loved
\q2 is the one I’ll call ‘my love’.
\q1
\v 26 In the place where they said,
\q2 ‘You’re not my people’
\q1 that’s where they’ll be called ‘children of the living God’.”
\m
\v 27 \add The prophet\add* Isaiah announced about Israel: ‘Even though the Israelites are as numerous as grains of sand, \add only\add* a minority \add of them\add* will be saved,
\v 28 because Yahweh will be abbreviating and terminating his message on the earth.’
\v 29 As Isaiah had previously written: ‘If Commander-in-chief Yahweh hadn’t left us \add some\add* descendants, we would have become like Sodom and Gomorrah’s \add people groups\add*.’
\s1 The Jews fail to believe
\p
\v 30 So then, what should we say? That non-Jews who weren’t godly reached out by faith to achieve righteousness,
\v 31 but Jews who chased after righteousness \add by obeying\add* The Law didn’t achieve it?
\v 32 How come? Because \add they tried to reach it\add* by actions and not by faith. They tripped over the stumbling stone,
\v 33 as it’s written:
\q1 ‘Look, I’m laying down a stumbling stone in Zion,
\q2 a rock \add that causes\add* offence.
\q1 Anyone who believes in him
\q2 won’t be disgraced.’
\c 10
\p
\v 1 Brothers and sisters, my heart would be very pleased indeed if Israelites would get rescued, and that’s what I request from God.
\v 2 I keep telling them that they’re \add very\add* zealous for God, yet they lack know-how.
\v 3 Because they didn’t know what God’s righteousness is, and they tried to establish their own \add system\add*, they weren’t covered by his righteousness.
\v 4 The fulfilment of The Law is Messiah, \add leading to\add* righteousness for everyone who believes.
\s1 Salvation is for all peoples
\p
\v 5 Moses wrote this about the Law’s righteousness: ‘The person who practices it will live because of it.’
\v 6 But the righteousness \add based on\add* faith says: ‘Don’t \add speculate\add* in your mind about who’ll go up to heaven in order to bring Messiah down,
\v 7 and don’t ask about who’ll go down to the pit in order to bring Messiah up.’
\v 8 So what does the righteousness \add based on\add* faith say? \add It says:\add* ‘The message is close to you—it’s in your mouth and heart.’ That’s the message of faith that we’ve been preaching:
\v 9 That if you speak out and acknowledge that Jesus is Yahweh, and you believe in your heart that God brought him back to life from the dead, you will be rescued,
\v 10 because it’s the belief in your mind that leads to righteousness, and it’s the acknowledgement from your mouth that leads to rescue.
\v 11 As the scriptures say: ‘Anyone who believes in him won’t \add end up\add* disgraced.’
\v 12 There’s no distinction between Jew and non-Jew, because \add we\add* all have the same master who gives generously to everyone who makes an appeal to him,
\v 13 because everyone who appeals to Yahweh’s authority will be rescued.
\p
\v 14 But how could they appeal to a god that they don’t \add even\add* believe in? And how could they believe if they haven’t \add even\add* heard \add about him\add*? And how would they hear if no one told \add them\add*?
\v 15 And who would be \add there\add* telling \add them\add* if no one was sent out? Compare what was written: ‘It’s so wonderful that these people came and announced the good message.’
\v 16 But not everyone accepted the good message, just like \add the prophet\add* Isaiah wrote: ‘Yahweh, who believed our report?’
\v 17 And so faith comes by hearing—listening to Messiah’s message.
\p
\v 18 But then I’d ask: Haven’t they heard \add it\add*? Indeed they have. \add It’s written:\add*
\q1 ‘Their voice went out into all the earth,
\q1 and their messages went across all the world.’
\m
\v 19 But \add again\add* I’d ask: Didn’t the Israelites know? \add Well,\add* firstly Moses wrote:
\q1 ‘I’ll be making you all jealous
\q2 using \add people\add* who are not even a nation.
\q1 Then using a foolish nation,
\q2 I’ll be making you all angry.’
\m
\v 20 And Isaiah dared to write:
\q1 ‘The ones who weren’t looking for me found me.
\q1 I showed myself to the ones not asking \add about me\add*.’
\m
\v 21 But to Israel he wrote: ‘All day I stretched out my hands to a people who won’t believe and who speak against \add me\add*.’
\c 11
\s1 God’s mercy on the Jews
\p
\v 1 Therefore I’m asking: Did God push his \add chosen\add* people away? Not at all, because I’m an Israelite, too, a descendant of Abraham in the Benjamin tribe.
\v 2 \add No,\add* God didn’t push away his people, who he knew before \add he chose them\add*. Or don’t you know what scripture says about Elijah pleading with God to deal with Israel? \add He said:\add*
\v 3 ‘Yahweh, they killed your prophets and tore down your altars, and I was left alone, and \add now\add* they want to kill me!’
\v 4 But what was revealed to him? \add God said:\add* ‘I’ve kept aside seven thousand others who haven’t worshipped Baal.’
\v 5 So too, at the present time, there’s a small proportion who’ve been chosen by God’s generosity.
\v 6 So if it’s by generosity, then it’s no longer by their actions, otherwise the generosity wouldn’t be generosity \add at all\add*.
\p
\v 7 What’s \add the conclusion\add* then? The Israelites didn’t find what they were looking for. Only the chosen ones reached it, and the rest were hardened,
\v 8 just like it was written:
\q1 ‘God gave them a lethargic spirit:
\q1 Eyes which can’t see and ears which can’t hear,
\q2 \add even\add* to this very day.’
\p
\v 9 And as David wrote:
\q1 ‘Let their table become a trap for them—
\q2 a temptation that will pay them back.
\q1
\v 10 Let their eyes be darkened so they can’t see,
\q1 and their backs be bent through all time.’
\p
\v 11 So now I ask: didn’t they stumble so that they would fall? Not at all. But because of their disobedience, salvation came to non-Jews in order to make Jews envious.
\v 12 But if their disobedience \add led to\add* gain for the \add rest of the\add* world, and if their loss \add led to\add* gain for the non-Jews, how much more their fullness?
\s1 The grafting in of non-Jews
\p
\v 13 But \add now\add* I’m speaking to you non-Jews. Since I am indeed an ambassador to the non-Jews, I take pride in my service,
\v 14 hoping that somehow I might make my fellow Jews envious and might save some of them.
\v 15 After all, if their rejection \add of the messiah\add* meant that \add the rest of\add* the world would be reconciled, what else could their acceptance bring other than coming back to life from the dead?
\p
\v 16 If the first portion \add of dough\add* is holy, so is the lump, and if the root is holy, so are the branches.
\v 17 But if some of the branches were broken off, and you \add non-Jews\add*, who are like a wild olive tree, were grafted into them, and so you too were able to share in the olive tree’s growth, which came from the root,
\v 18 then don’t brag that you’re better than the branches. If you must boast, remember that you’re not supporting the root, but the root is supporting you.
\p
\v 19 \add Ah,\add* but then you’ll say, ‘Branches were broken off so that we could be grafted in.’
\v 20 \add Well\add* you’re right. They were broken off because of their disbelief, and \add it’s true\add* that you’re there because of your faith. But don’t get a big head—maintain your reverence \add for God\add*,
\v 21 because if he didn’t spare the natural branches \add when they showed disbelief\add*, then he won’t spare you either.
\v 22 So you can see both God’s kindness and his severity—those who disobeyed \add experienced\add* his severity, but you \add experienced\add* his kindness. Otherwise you’ll be pruned off, too,
\v 23 and also, if they turn away from their disbelief, they’ll be grafted \add back\add* in, because God has the power to do that.
\v 24 If you were cut off an olive tree that’s \em wild\em* by nature, and contrary to normal practice were grafted into a \em cultivated\em* olive tree, how much more \add easily\add* would those who naturally belong be grafted into their own olive tree!
\s1 God’s mercy is for everyone
\p
\v 25 Brothers and sisters, I don’t want you to be ignorant of this mystery, otherwise you might end up becoming proud: There’s a partial hardening coming to the Jews until the fullness of the non-Jews comes,
\v 26 and then all the Jews will be saved, as it’s been written:
\q1 ‘The rescuer will be coming out of Zion.
\q1 He will drive ungodliness away from Jacob.
\q1
\v 27 And this is my agreement with them,
\q1 whenever I might take away their sins.’
\m
\v 28 Concerning the good message, \add the Jews are\add* enemies for your sake, but concerning being chosen, \add they’re\add* loved because of their ancestors,
\v 29 because God’s gifts and his invitation are irrevocable.
\v 30 You \add non-Jews\add* didn’t originally believe in God, but now you’ve been shown mercy because of the disbelief of these Jews.
\v 31 Those Jews disbelieved so that as you \add receive\add* mercy, they can also now be shown mercy,
\v 32 because God imprisoned them all in disbelief so that he could show mercy to everyone.
\s1 Everything comes from God
\p
\v 33 \add Yes,\add* God’s wisdom and knowledge are both so richly deep. His judgements can’t be understood and his paths can’t be mapped,
\v 34 because:
\q1 ‘Who can know Yahweh’s mind
\q1 or become his counsellor?’
\q1
\v 35 ‘Who has been able to give him something,
\q2 →so that \add God\add* \add now\add* needs to repay them?’
\m
\v 36 \add Yes,\add* because everything comes from God, and everything comes through him and to him. May he be honoured throughout the ages. May it be so.
\c 12
\s1 Different ways to serve ‘the body’
\p
\v 1 So then, brothers and sisters, with compassion from God I urge you to present your bodies as a living sacrifice—holy \add and\add* pleasing to God—your logical \add and\add* sacred service.
\v 2 Don’t conform to \add the norms of\add* this age, but be transformed by the renewing of your minds, then you’ll be able to \add accept and\add* approve God’s good, pleasing, and perfect intentions.
\p
\v 3 Because of the generosity which has been shown to me, I’m saying that none of you should think of yourself more highly than what’s appropriate, but rather \add you should\add* be sensible about what you think of yourselves according to how much faith God’s allotted to each of you.
\v 4 Just like one body has many components, and each individual component works differently,
\v 5 so too we’re all part of one body in Messiah and we’re all parts of each other.
\v 6 \add We\add* have gifts according to the special generosity that was shown to us: if it’s prophecy, \add then do it\add* in proportion to your faith,
\v 7 or if it’s service, \add then\add* serve, or teaching, \add then\add* teach,
\v 8 or encouraging, \add then\add* encourage, or sharing, \add then\add* be generous, or leading, \add then do it\add* earnestly, or showing mercy, \add then do it\add* cheerfully.
\p
\v 9 Your love is to be free from hypocrisy. Deplore evil, and be united in doing good.
\v 10 Be devoted to one another in brotherly love, preferring to honour one another \add above yourselves\add*.
\v 11 Be diligent, rather than reluctant, with a spirit that’s zealous to serve the master.
\v 12 Be cheerful about your hope, enduring persecution and consistently praying.
\v 13 Contribute to the needs of the believers and practice hospitality.
\p
\v 14 Bless anyone who persecutes you—be a blesser and not a curser.
\v 15 Be cheerful along with others who are happy, but weep along with those who are weeping.
\v 16 Treat others equally—not treating some as higher, and being condescending to those who are poorer. Don’t act in a calculating way.
\p
\v 17 Don’t pay back anyone’s evil with evil, but do good to everyone.
\v 18 If it’s possible for you, \add brothers and sisters\add*, be at peace with everyone.
\v 19 Dear friends, don’t take revenge on others, but leave it to God’s severe anger, because it’s written: ‘Yahweh says, Vengeance belongs to me—I’ll be the one to repay.’
\v 20 But if your enemies are hungry, then feed them, and if they’re thirsty, then give them a drink, because you’ll be leaving them to their own shame and judgement.
\v 21 Don’t be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good.
\c 13
\s1 Respect authorities and pay tax
\p
\v 1 Everyone should be subject to the authorities who are over them, because there’s no authority except through God, and they’re appointed by God.
\v 2 So anyone opposing authority has rebelled against what God has instituted, and everyone who rebels is bringing judgement on themselves,
\v 3 because rulers don’t evoke fear in \add people\add* doing good, just those doing evil. Oh, so you don’t want to be afraid of the authorities? Then do good, and you’ll receive praise from them \add instead\add*,
\v 4 because they’re God’s servants for your good. But if you’re doing evil, then you should \add rightly\add* be afraid, because the authorities aren’t armed without reason—they’re God’s servants to dispense severe anger to people doing evil.
\v 5 Hence it’s necessary to be subject to them, not only \add to avoid\add* their severe anger, but also because of your consciences.
\p
\v 6 That’s also why you \add believers\add* should pay the taxes that you owe, because \add the authorities\add* are God’s servants, and they work hard at it.
\v 7 So fulfil all of your obligations: if it’s tax then pay it, or revenue then give it, or if it’s respect then show it, or giving honour then do it.
\s1 Love and obedience
\p
\v 8 Don’t be in debt to anyone, other than the debt of love to each other, because people who love each other have fulfilled God’s law,
\v 9 which is: Don’t commit adultery. Don’t murder. Don’t steal. Don’t covet \add what others have\add*. All of those, plus any other commands, can be summarised as: Love your neighbour like you love yourself.
\v 10 If you love people near to you, you won’t do evil to them, thus love fulfills God’s law.
\p
\v 11 And since you \add believers\add* understand current events, it’s now time to get up from your sleep, because the time for our rescue is nearer now than it was when we first believed.
\v 12 The night has progressed and the day is getting closer, so turn away from actions done in darkness and put on the armour of the light.
\v 13 In the daytime we have to walk decently—not carousing \add on the street\add* and being drunk, not having sex and acting lustfully, and not causing strife and acting jealously.
\v 14 Instead, dress yourselves in the master Jesus Messiah, and don’t give in to physical lusts and desires.
\c 14
\s1 Don’t condemn each other
\p
\v 1 Accept others who are faltering in their faith, without highlighting their wrong ideas.
\v 2 Some people think it’s ok to eat anything, while others who are faltering will only eat vegetables.
\v 3 The one who eats anything shouldn’t despise the other, and the one who doesn’t eat \add meat\add* shouldn’t condemn the other, because \add both\add* are accepted by God.
\v 4 Who are you to judge someone else’s house servants? Their own master will judge if they stand or fall, and they’ll stand because the master is able to \add help\add* them.
\p
\v 5 \add Similarly,\add* some people consider one day to be more important than some other day, yet others consider every day to be alike. Each \add person\add* should be fully convinced in their own mind.
\v 6 Anyone who considers a \add certain\add* day to be special is thinking about Yahweh. \add Likewise,\add* anyone who’s eating \add meat\add* is eating to \add serve\add* Yahweh, and gives thanks to God. The person who doesn’t eat \add meat\add* is declining \add it in order to serve\add* Yahweh, and gives thanks to God.
\v 7 After all, we don’t live for ourselves, and we don’t die for ourselves.
\v 8 If we keep living, then we’re living for the master, and if we die, then we’re dying for the master, so whether we live or die, we belong to the master,
\v 9 because Messiah died and came back to life in order to be master of both the dead and the living.
\v 10 So why are you judging fellow believers? And why are you also scorning them? After all, we’ll all \add end up\add* standing in God’s court of judgement.
\v 11 As it’s been written:
\q1 “Yahweh is telling me, ‘I am alive
\q2 and every person will bow to me,
\q1 and each one will confess to God.’ ”
\m
\v 12 So consequently, each of us will \add need\add* to give an account of ourselves to God.
\s1 Taking care not to make others fall
\p
\v 13 So we shouldn’t be judging each other, but instead we should be judging \add ourselves\add*—that we’re not causing another believer to stumble or \add fall into\add* temptation.
\v 14 I know (and I’m \add totally\add* persuaded) that in master Jesus there’s no \add object\add* that’s ‘immoral’\f + \fr 14:14 \ft Or (traditionally) ‘unclean’.\f* in itself, but it only becomes ‘immoral’ to the person who evaluates it as such.
\v 15 If a fellow believer is upset because of the food \add you eat\add*, then you’re no longer acting in love. Don’t destroy \add the faith of\add* someone that Messiah died for, \add just\add* for the sake of food.
\v 16 Don’t let anyone slander the good that you do.
\v 17 God’s kingdom doesn’t \add consist of\add* food and drink, but \add of\add* righteousness, peace, and happiness in the holy spirit,
\v 18 so anyone serving the messiah in those ways pleases God and is approved by the people.
\p
\v 19 So then, we should be pursuing actions that lead to peace and things that build each other up—
\v 20 don’t tear down God’s work \add just\add* on account of food. Indeed, all foods are ‘clean,’\f + \fr 14:20 \ft In Hebrew, ‘kosher’.\f* but it’s wrong for a person to eat anything that \add causes someone else\add* to stumble.
\v 21 It’s good to avoid eating meat or drinking wine or \add doing\add* anything else that \add might\add* cause your fellow believer to stumble or be offended or fall.
\v 22 \add That way,\add* you keep your faith privately, between yourself and God. \add God\add* will bless anyone who doesn’t bring guilt on themselves by doing something that they’ve determined to be right.
\v 23 But for anyone who doubts that it’s ok, if they eat it, then they’ve condemned themselves, because they’re not acting in faith, and everything that’s not done in faith is sin.
\c 15
\s1 Living to please others
\p
\v 1 We who are powerful should be carrying the weaknesses of the powerless, rather than \add just\add* trying to please ourselves.
\v 2 Each one of us should be pleasing those near us—displaying goodness and building \add them\add* up,
\v 3 because even the messiah didn’t please himself, but, as it was written: ‘Those who were insulting you, their insults landed on me.’
\v 4 Everything that was written in the past was written for teaching us, so that through endurance and through encouragement from the scriptures, we would have hope.
\v 5 May the God of endurance and encouragement help you all to think likewise, just like Jesus Messiah did,
\v 6 so that you’ll be united in your purpose and your teaching, thus honouring the God and father of our master, Jesus Messiah.
\s1 The good message for non-Jews
\p
\v 7 So then, accept each other just as the messiah accepted you, so that God will be honoured.
\v 8 I can tell you that Messiah became a servant of the circumcised \add Jews\add* so that God’s truth \add will be seen by the\add* confirmation of the promises that were made to our ancestors,
\v 9 and \add also a servant\add* for the non-Jews so that they’ll honour God for his mercy, as it was written:
\q1 ‘Because of this I’ll be telling the pagans about you,
\q1 and I’ll be singing praises to your name.’
\m
\v 10 \add In a different place\add* it’s written:
\q1 ‘You non-Jews, be happy along with his people.’
\m
\v 11 And again:
\q1 ‘Praise Yahweh you non-Jews,
\q1 and let all the peoples \add of the world\add* praise him.’
\m
\v 12 Also Isaiah wrote:
\q1 ‘There will be a descendant of Jesse,
\q1 and the one coming to rule over non-Jews—
\q2 the non-Jews will put their hope in him.’
\p
\v 13 May the God of hope that you believe in fill you \add believers\add* with happiness and peace, so that your hope in the power of \add the\add* holy spirit will \add grow to be\add* plentiful.
\s1 Paul as missionary to non-Jews
\p
\v 14 My brothers and sisters, I’ve also been persuaded about you myself, that having been filled with knowledge, you’re also full of goodness and able to rebuke each other.
\v 15 But I wrote to you more boldly, partly to remind you, \add brothers and sisters,\add* because of the generosity God showed me
\v 16 so that I’d serve the non-Jews for Messiah Jesus, sharing God’s good message so that the offering of the non-Jews would be acceptable \add to him\add*, having been purified by \add the\add* holy spirit. WHAT DOES THIS MEAN?
\v 17 Therefore I’m boasting in Messiah Jesus about things \add to do with\add* God.
\v 18 I wouldn’t dare to speak about anything except what Messiah did through me, which led to the obedience of non-Jews through my message and \add through my\add* doing
\v 19 powerful miracles and amazing things in the power of God’s spirit, so that from Jerusalem all the way around to Illyricum, I’ve obeyed the Messiah’s good message.
\v 20 I was eager to be preaching the good message wherever Messiah wasn’t known about, so that I wouldn’t be building on someone else’s foundations,
\v 21 but as it was written:
\q1 ‘Anyone who hasn’t been told about him,
\q2 they will be seeing,
\q1 and anyone who hasn’t heard about him,
\q2 they will be understanding.’
\s1 Paul’s desire to visit Rome
\p
\v 22 That’s also why I wasn’t able to come \add to see\add* you \add Romans,\add*
\v 23 but now I no longer have a role in those regions. I’ve been wanting to come there for many years now—
\v 24 whenever I’m able to go to Spain next, I’m hoping to see you on my way, and then after \add spending time\add* with you \add Romans\add*, you’ll be able to send me on to my destination.
\v 25 But for now, I’m going to Jerusalem to serve the believers there.
\v 26 \add The believers in\add* Macedonia and Achaia took pleasure in making a contribution for the poor \add people\add* among the believers \add there\add* in Jerusalem.
\v 27 They took pleasure, \add but\add* they’re also in debt to them, because if the non-Jews have shared in Jewish spiritual things, then they should also support them with material things.
\v 28 So then, having done that, and having guaranteed \add heavenly rewards\add* for them, I’ll go to Spain by way of your \add place\add*,
\v 29 and I know that when I come, I’ll come \add bearing\add* Messiah’s full blessing.
\p
\v 30 So brothers and sisters, I’m urging you by our master Jesus Messiah and by the spirit’s love, to strive along with me in praying to God for me—
\v 31 that I’ll escape from \add the schemes of\add* those in Judea who refuse to believe. Also pray that my service in Jerusalem would be acceptable to the believers there
\v 32 so that by God’s will, I can happily come to your place and be refreshed \add there\add* with you, \add brothers and sisters\add*.
\v 33 \add May\add* the God of peace be with all of you. May it be so.
\c 16
\s1 Various personal greetings
\p
\v 1 I’m commending our sister Phoebe to you, \add brothers and sisters\add*—she’s a servant in the assembly at Cenchrea.
\v 2 You can accept her in \add the\add* master as worthy among godly people, and help her with anything she needs, because she’s been a supporter of many of us, including myself.
\p
\v 3 Greet Priscilla and Aquila, my workmates in Messiah Jesus,
\v 4 who risked their lives to save mine. It’s not just me who’s thankful for them, but also all the assemblies of the non-Jews
\v 5 and the assembly \add that meets\add* in their house.
\p Greet my dear \add friend\add* Epainetos, who was one of the first in Asia \add Minor province\add* to \add follow\add* Messiah.
\v 6 Greet Mary, who worked hard for you, \add brothers and sisters\add*.
\v 7 Greet Andronicus and \add our sister\add* Junia, my fellow citizens and fellow prisoners with me, who are notable among the ambassadors in Messiah who \add started\add* before me.
\p
\v 8 Greet Ampliatus, my dear \add friend\add* in \add the\add* master.
\v 9 Greet Urbanus, our fellow worker in Messiah, and my dear \add friend\add* Stachys.
\v 10 Greet Apelles, who’s a proven follower of Messiah. Greet those from Aristobulus’s \add household\add*.
\v 11 Greet Herodion, my fellow citizen. Greet those in Narcissus’s \add household\add* who are in \add the\add* master.
\p
\v 12 Greet Tryphena and Tryphosa, those \add women\add* who are working for \add the\add* master. Greet my dear \add friend\add* Persis, another woman who’s worked for \add the\add* master a lot.
\v 13 Greet Rufus, who’s chosen by \add the\add* master, and his mother, who also mothered me.
\v 14 Greet Asyncritus, Phlegon, Hermes, Patrobas, Hermas, and the other brothers and sisters with them.
\v 15 Greet Philologus, Julia, Nereus and his sister, and Olympas, and all the godly people \add there\add* with them.
\p
\v 16 Greet each other with a traditional kiss. All the messiah’s assemblies \add here\add* send their greetings to you, \add brothers and sisters\add*.
\s1 The ending warnings
\p
\v 17 Brothers and sisters, I urge you to beware of those who are \add always\add* talking about faults and tempting you with things that are against what you’ve been taught. Turn away from them,
\v 18 because such people are serving their own bellies instead of our master Messiah, and they’re deceiving innocent people with their smooth speech and ‘blessings’.
\v 19 I’m excited because the \add story\add* of your obedience has travelled far, but I want you \add believers\add* to \add remain\add* wise about what’s good, and innocent about evil,
\v 20 then the God of peace will quickly crush Satan under your feet.
\s1 Final greetings
\p \add May\add* our master Jesus’ generosity be with you \add believers\add*.
\p
\v 21 My co-worker Timothy sends his greetings, as do my fellow citizens Luke, Jason, and Sosipater.
\p
\v 22 I, Tertius, the one writing down this letter, greet you \add believers\add* in \add the\add* master.
\p
\v 23 My host Gaius and the whole assembly \add here\add* greet you \add believers\add*.
\s1 Closing
\p
\v 24 ◘
\v 25 \add There’s\add* someone who, in keeping with my preaching of the good message and the declaration about Jesus Messiah, is able to strengthen you \add believers\add* in keeping with the revelation of the mystery that had been kept secret through the ages.
\v 26 But he’s been \add there\add* in the prophetic scriptures, and now he’s been revealed in keeping with the eternal God’s command, and been made known to all the nations for faithful obedience.
\v 27 \add Now\add* may the only wise God be honoured through Jesus Messiah throughout the \add remaining\add* ages. May it be so.
